<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE HermitCrabInput SYSTEM "HermitCrabInput.dtd">
<!-- Positive and negative controls for machine#504 ("Exact feature inversion"), companion to
     edge-cases/chained-output-feature-override-loss (the deliberate red for that issue). That
     fixture pins ONE known loss (a rule's own OutputHeadFeatures re-overwriting a feature an
     EARLIER rule already wrote, then the earlier rule's own re-unification failing). This fixture
     is the adequacy argument the issue's remaining work asks for: it exercises the OTHER shapes
     exact feature inversion has to get right, each independently forward-synthesized and checked
     against the real oracle rather than sketched algebraically.

     Three head features, all nested one level under the complex feature cfAgr (agr.num, agr.pers,
     agr.cls), so every check below is a NESTED feature path, never a bare top-level one:
       mrNum   (rule)    OutputHeadFeatures agr.num=pl.  Two DISJUNCTIVE subrules choose the affix
                         allomorph ("-i" after a consonant-final stem, "-u" after a vowel-final
                         one); both write the SAME OutputHeadFeatures regardless of which fires.
       mrNumSg (rule)    OutputHeadFeatures agr.num=sg via a THIRD, unconstrained affix ("-e"). Not
                         part of the mrNum/mrPers/mrGate chain conceptually; it exists solely to
                         give agr.num a genuine CONFLICTING value to test mrGate against (see
                         eSim's simeao below), the same role eDam's agr.cls=clsB already plays for
                         agr.cls.
       mrPers  (rule)    OutputHeadFeatures agr.pers=1. Unconstrained; contributes a DIFFERENT
                         nested path than mrNum, never touching agr.num.
       mrGate  (rule)    RequiredHeadFeatures agr.num=pl AND agr.pers=1 AND agr.cls=clsA, all three
                         checked TOGETHER against whatever the derivation has accumulated so far.
                         This is UNIFICATION, not presence-checking: a path with no value at all is
                         compatible with any requirement and mrGate accepts it (see eBas's basiao
                         below, and simio/simao, which skip mrNum or mrPers outright and still pass
                         mrGate); a path that already holds a CONFLICTING concrete value is what
                         actually causes a refusal (eDam's clsB, eSim's simeao via mrNumSg's sg).
                         num/pers reaching mrGate as pl/1 at all, when both mrNum and mrPers ran,
                         is still a real MERGE of two rules' independent writes into the one
                         nested cfAgr structure (neither rule alone provides both); cls surviving
                         from the root's own AssignedHeadFeatures to mrGate, untouched by any rule
                         in between, is the PASSTHROUGH check.
     No two rules ever write the SAME nested path, so this fixture does not re-probe the override-
     loss mechanism chained-output-feature-override-loss already pins; it probes MERGE (do two
     independent writes combine?), PASSTHROUGH (does an unwritten value survive?), and the
     unify-vs-absent distinction above, which are logically separate failure modes for an
     exact-inversion implementation.

     A GENUINE GAP, stated here rather than papered over: the task that produced this fixture asked
     for "a feature variable (alpha-style agreement between RequiredHeadFeatures and
     OutputHeadFeatures)". That construct cannot be written in grammar.xml at all, for a hard
     structural reason, not a coverage gap in this fixture:
       - VariableFeature/AlphaVariable (HermitCrabInput.dtd, the SimpleContext/AlphaVariables and
         MorphologicalSubrule/VariableFeatures productions) exists, but
         XmlLanguageLoader.LoadVariables (src/SIL.Machine.Morphology.HermitCrab/
         XmlLanguageLoader.cs) resolves every VariableFeature's "phonologicalFeature" IDREF with
         `_language.PhonologicalFeatureSystem.GetFeature<SymbolicFeature>(...)`, hardcoded to the
         PHONOLOGICAL feature system used by segments. There is no code path that resolves a
         VariableFeature against `_language.SyntacticFeatureSystem` (the HeadFeatures/FootFeatures
         registry), so a VariableFeature can never name a head feature.
       - FeatureValue — the only element RequiredHeadFeatures/OutputHeadFeatures/
         AssignedHeadFeatures ever contain — has exactly two relevant attributes in the DTD:
         `feature` (an IDREF to the feature) and `symbolValues` (IDREFS to concrete Symbol values).
         There is no variable/binding attribute at all. XmlLanguageLoader.LoadFeatureStruct (same
         file) confirms this at the implementation level: it either reads concrete `symbolValues`
         into a SymbolicFeatureValue or, if the referenced feature is a ComplexFeature, recurses;
         there is no third branch that produces a bound (unify-by-name) variable value.
     So alpha-style agreement between a RequiredHeadFeatures and a later OutputHeadFeatures — the
     PC-PATR-style "these two positions must come out equal, whichever value that turns out to be"
     — is not expressible through this XML front end for head/foot features, full stop. The closest
     analog the format supports is what mrGate's agr.cls check above does: a value that is assigned
     once (here, on the lexical entry, since no rule can conditionally re-assign a symbol per
     branch) and never overwritten, so a correct implementation must carry it through untouched.
     eDam's own agr.cls=clsB is the same construct with the OTHER concrete value, verifying this is
     real value-tracking and not a hardcoded clsA check. This is a real analog, not a substitute for
     the variable construct — it cannot show that TWO DIFFERENT rule-written positions are forced
     to the same value only because they share a variable name; it can only show that ONE value
     already fixed at the root survives. See the words.yaml header for how each word is verified.

     sources: https://github.com/sillsdev/machine/issues/504 -->
<HermitCrabInput>
  <Language>
    <Name>SyntacticFeatureInversionControls</Name>
    <PartsOfSpeech>
      <PartOfSpeech id="posN"><Name>n</Name></PartOfSpeech>
    </PartsOfSpeech>
    <HeadFeatures>
      <ComplexFeature id="cfAgr"><Name>agr</Name></ComplexFeature>
      <SymbolicFeature id="fNum">
        <Name>num</Name>
        <Symbols>
          <Symbol id="numPl">pl</Symbol>
          <Symbol id="numSg">sg</Symbol>
        </Symbols>
      </SymbolicFeature>
      <SymbolicFeature id="fPers">
        <Name>pers</Name>
        <Symbols><Symbol id="persA">1</Symbol></Symbols>
      </SymbolicFeature>
      <SymbolicFeature id="fCls">
        <Name>cls</Name>
        <Symbols>
          <Symbol id="clsA">a</Symbol>
          <Symbol id="clsB">b</Symbol>
        </Symbols>
      </SymbolicFeature>
    </HeadFeatures>
    <CharacterDefinitionTable id="tbl">
      <Name>Main</Name>
      <SegmentDefinitions>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cS"><Representations><Representation>s</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cI"><Representations><Representation>i</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cM"><Representations><Representation>m</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cA"><Representations><Representation>a</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cD"><Representations><Representation>d</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cO"><Representations><Representation>o</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cU"><Representations><Representation>u</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cT"><Representations><Representation>t</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cB"><Representations><Representation>b</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
        <SegmentDefinition id="cE"><Representations><Representation>e</Representation></Representations></SegmentDefinition>
      </SegmentDefinitions>
    </CharacterDefinitionTable>
    <NaturalClasses>
      <FeatureNaturalClass id="ncAny"><Name>Any</Name></FeatureNaturalClass>
      <!-- Not a complete phonological consonant/vowel inventory — just the two segments this
           fixture's roots actually end in, named for what mrNum's disjunctive pair checks. "t" (see
           eDat below) is deliberately in NEITHER class: it is the "belongs to neither disjunct"
           control. -->
      <SegmentNaturalClass id="ncConsEnding"><Name>consEnding</Name><Segment segment="cS" /><Segment segment="cM" /></SegmentNaturalClass>
      <SegmentNaturalClass id="ncVowEnding"><Name>vowEnding</Name><Segment segment="cO" /></SegmentNaturalClass>
    </NaturalClasses>
    <Strata>
      <!-- morphologicalRuleOrder="linear": makes the mrNum-before-mrPers-before-mrGate chain
           unambiguous by construction, same reasoning and precedent as
           edge-cases/chained-output-feature-override-loss. fieldworks_producible is false for the
           same reason that fixture gives (words.yaml has the full citation): HCLoader hardcodes
           Unordered at every Stratum it builds and can never emit "linear". -->
      <Stratum characterDefinitionTable="tbl" morphologicalRuleOrder="linear"
               morphologicalRules="mrNum mrNumSg mrPers mrGate">
        <Name>Main</Name>
        <MorphologicalRuleDefinitions>
          <!-- mrNum: the DISJUNCTIVE ALLOMORPH PAIR. subNumCons ("-i") requires a consonant-ending
               stem; subNumVow ("-u") requires a vowel-ending stem. The two are mutually exclusive by
               environment (no stem in this fixture's lexicon satisfies both, or neither, except
               eDat, whose final "t" satisfies neither on purpose — see eDat below). Either subrule
               that fires writes the SAME OutputHeadFeatures: inversion must recover agr.num=pl
               regardless of which allomorph the surface form used. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrNum" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>num</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subNumCons">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemNumCons"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><CopyFromInput index="stemNumCons" /><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>i</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments></MorphologicalOutput>
                <RequiredEnvironments>
                  <Environment><LeftEnvironment><PhoneticTemplate><PhoneticSequence><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncConsEnding" /></PhoneticSequence></PhoneticTemplate></LeftEnvironment></Environment>
                </RequiredEnvironments>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subNumVow">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemNumVow"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><CopyFromInput index="stemNumVow" /><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>u</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments></MorphologicalOutput>
                <RequiredEnvironments>
                  <Environment><LeftEnvironment><PhoneticTemplate><PhoneticSequence><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncVowEnding" /></PhoneticSequence></PhoneticTemplate></LeftEnvironment></Environment>
                </RequiredEnvironments>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <OutputHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fNum" symbolValues="numPl" /></FeatureValue></OutputHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>NUM</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>PL</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- mrNumSg: unconstrained, single affix "-e". Writes agr.num=sg, the CONFLICTING value
               against mrNum's agr.num=pl. Its only job is giving mrGate a real value to refuse
               (see eSim's simeao below), the same role eDam's own agr.cls=clsB already plays. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrNumSg" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>numSg</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subNumSg">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemNumSg"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><CopyFromInput index="stemNumSg" /><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>e</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <OutputHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fNum" symbolValues="numSg" /></FeatureValue></OutputHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>NUMSG</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>SG</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- mrPers: unconstrained. Writes agr.pers=1, a DIFFERENT nested path than mrNum's
               agr.num, so it contributes rather than overwrites when both apply to the same word. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrPers" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>pers</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subPers">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemPers"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><CopyFromInput index="stemPers" /><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>a</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <OutputHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fPers" symbolValues="persA" /></FeatureValue></OutputHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>PERS</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>1</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
          <!-- mrGate: the MERGE + PASSTHROUGH discriminator. Requires all three nested paths at
               once: agr.num=pl (mrNum's own write, or mrNumSg's CONFLICTING sg), agr.pers=1
               (mrPers's own write), agr.cls=clsA (no rule ever writes this — only a lexical
               entry's own AssignedHeadFeatures does). This is a unification check: a path with NO
               value reaching mrGate (mrNum or mrPers skipped; eBas's root, which never assigns
               cls) is compatible with the requirement and does not block the parse. Only a path
               that already holds a genuinely DIFFERENT concrete value (eDam's clsB, mrNumSg's sg)
               causes a real refusal. See words.yaml for which of this fixture's words land on
               which side of that distinction. -->
          <MorphologicalRule id="mrGate" requiredPartsOfSpeech="posN" outputPartOfSpeech="posN">
            <Name>gate</Name>
            <MorphologicalSubrules>
              <MorphologicalSubrule id="subGate">
                <MorphologicalInput><PhoneticSequence id="stemGate"><OptionalSegmentSequence min="1" max="-1"><SimpleContext naturalClass="ncAny" /></OptionalSegmentSequence></PhoneticSequence></MorphologicalInput>
                <MorphologicalOutput><CopyFromInput index="stemGate" /><InsertSegments><PhoneticShape>o</PhoneticShape></InsertSegments></MorphologicalOutput>
              </MorphologicalSubrule>
            </MorphologicalSubrules>
            <RequiredHeadFeatures>
              <FeatureValue feature="cfAgr">
                <FeatureValue feature="fNum" symbolValues="numPl" />
                <FeatureValue feature="fPers" symbolValues="persA" />
                <FeatureValue feature="fCls" symbolValues="clsA" />
              </FeatureValue>
            </RequiredHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>GATE</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>GATE</Gloss>
          </MorphologicalRule>
        </MorphologicalRuleDefinitions>
        <LexicalEntries>
          <!-- eSim: consonant-ending root (agr.cls=clsA), the fixture's main positive-control root.
               Exercises mrNum's subNumCons ("-i") arm of the disjunctive pair. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eSim" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aSim"><PhoneticShape>sim</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <AssignedHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fCls" symbolValues="clsA" /></FeatureValue></AssignedHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>SIM</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>sim</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- eDo: vowel-ending root (agr.cls=clsA), the disjunctive pair's OTHER arm. Exercises
               mrNum's subNumVow ("-u"). Same cls value as eSim, so the only thing that differs
               between eSim's and eDo's full chains is which allomorph mrNum used — isolating the
               disjunctive-pair variable from the merge/passthrough variables. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eDo" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aDo"><PhoneticShape>do</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <AssignedHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fCls" symbolValues="clsA" /></FeatureValue></AssignedHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>DO</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>do</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- eDam: consonant-ending root, agr.cls=clsB (the OTHER concrete value). Isolates the
               cls passthrough check: everything else about its chain is identical to eSim's, so a
               failure at mrGate can only be attributed to cls. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eDam" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aDam"><PhoneticShape>dam</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <AssignedHeadFeatures><FeatureValue feature="cfAgr"><FeatureValue feature="fCls" symbolValues="clsB" /></FeatureValue></AssignedHeadFeatures>
            <MorphemeId>DAM</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>dam</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- eBas: consonant-ending root with NO AssignedHeadFeatures at all — agr.cls is
               genuinely UNSET, not merely a different value from eDam's. Distinguishes "wrong value
               present" (eDam's clsB, which conflicts and mrGate refuses) from "no value present"
               (eBas, which unifies with anything and mrGate ACCEPTS) — verified against the real
               oracle, not assumed; see words.yaml's basiao entry for the full write-up of why this
               is correct unification behaviour, not a bug. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eBas" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aBas"><PhoneticShape>bas</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>BAS</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>bas</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
          <!-- eDat: ends in "t", which is in NEITHER ncConsEnding NOR ncVowEnding. Non-vacuousness
               control for the disjunctive pair: proves mrNum does not default to either allomorph
               when its environment condition genuinely is not met by either arm. -->
          <LexicalEntry id="eDat" partOfSpeech="posN">
            <Allomorphs><Allomorph id="aDat"><PhoneticShape>dat</PhoneticShape></Allomorph></Allomorphs>
            <MorphemeId>DAT</MorphemeId>
            <Gloss>dat</Gloss>
          </LexicalEntry>
        </LexicalEntries>
      </Stratum>
    </Strata>
  </Language>
</HermitCrabInput>
